<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/morphoeval-delphi10000-ceratites-nodosus-turing-wavelength-s0> a pk:Claim ;
    rdfs:label "Ceratites nodosus Schnakenberg Turing wavenumber at 26 µm (Ladinian, 240 Ma) (Ceratites nodosus)",
        "Ceratites nodosus Schnakenberg Turing wavenumber at 26 µm (Ladinian, 240 Ma) faithfully reproduces Ceratites nodosus morphology" ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/delphi10000-ceratites-nodosus-turing-wavelength-s0-1>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/delphi10000-ceratites-nodosus-turing-wavelength-s0-2>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/delphi10000-ceratites-nodosus-turing-wavelength-s0-3> ;
    dct:description "The simulation script(s) models/2026-delphi10000-ceratites-nodosus-turing-wavelength-s0.py implement the cited source model as published and produce forms that match real Ceratites nodosus specimens and the source authors' figures. A belief under audit, not a fact: its credibility is carried by the MorphoEval rubric assessments." ;
    dct:subject <https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/main/models/2026-delphi10000-ceratites-nodosus-turing-wavelength-s0.py> ;
    schema:about "Ceratites nodosus" ;
    schema:additionalProperty "does_not_claim: That Boyajian and Lutz reported D_f = 1.34 for this specimen.",
        "does_not_claim: That the 1.088 mm sibling slot's diffusivities were reused.",
        "does_not_claim: That the Schnakenberg rates were measured in a Ceratites mantle.",
        "does_not_claim: That this photograph, or any published SEM of Ceratites nodosus, resolved a 26 µm pigment wavelength.",
        "reconstructed_in_script: 96 by 96 periodic explicit Euler integration and a radial-FFT wavelength.",
        "reconstructed_in_script: Shell counts N = L / lambda at the campaign shell sizes.",
        "reconstructed_in_script: a = 0.062, b = 1.140, gamma = 1.15 times onset, Du and Dv solved for lambda = 2.56e-05 m.",
        "reported_in_source: Boyajian and Lutz (1992) treat ammonoid suture complexity with a fractal descriptor. Campaign D_f values are 1.18, 1.34, and 1.55.",
        "reported_in_source: Meinhardt and Klingler (1987) interpret molluscan shell pigment as an activator-inhibitor pattern.",
        "reported_in_source: Schnakenberg (1979) kinetics f = a - u + u^2 v, g = b - u^2 v.",
        "reported_in_source: Turing (1952) dispersion relation and diffusion-driven instability.",
        "transfer_boundary: The 26 µm field is a mantle-epithelial scale. Mapping its chroma onto the shell photograph is a visualisation, not a to-scale pigment restoration." ;
    pk:anatomicalStructure "Turing pigment stripe / ocellus wavelength in Ceratites nodosus (Middle Triassic (Ladinian, 240 Ma))" ;
    pk:aristotelianEfficientCause "1. Chemical Turing / RD" ;
    pk:aristotelianMegistonGenos "II. Anaima (Invertebrata)" ;
    pk:aristotelianPoionForm "Stripes, Spots & Ocelli" ;
    pk:aristotelianPosonLaw "Turing Wavenumber k_c" ;
    pk:aristotelianSubalternClass "Ostrakoderma (Mollusca & Echinodermata)" ;
    pk:biologicalGenus "Ceratites" ;
    pk:context "script(s): https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/main/models/2026-delphi10000-ceratites-nodosus-turing-wavelength-s0.py; output: docs/media/ceratites_nodosus_turing_wavelength_s0_comparison.png; organism: Ceratites nodosus; environment: python 3.12.3, numpy 2.5.4, scipy 1.18.1, matplotlib 3.11.2, Pillow 13.0.0; claim_boundary: [reported_in_source: Turing (1952) dispersion relation and diffusion-driven instability. | Schnakenberg (1979) kinetics f = a - u + u^2 v, g = b - u^2 v. | Meinhardt and Klingler (1987) interpret molluscan shell pigment as an activator-inhibitor pattern. | Boyajian and Lutz (1992) treat ammonoid suture complexity with a fractal descriptor. Campaign D_f values are 1.18, 1.34, and 1.55.; reconstructed_in_script: a = 0.062, b = 1.140, gamma = 1.15 times onset, Du and Dv solved for lambda = 2.56e-05 m. | 96 by 96 periodic explicit Euler integration and a radial-FFT wavelength. | Shell counts N = L / lambda at the campaign shell sizes.; transfer_boundary: The 26 µm field is a mantle-epithelial scale. Mapping its chroma onto the shell photograph is a visualisation, not a to-scale pigment restoration.; does_not_claim: That this photograph, or any published SEM of Ceratites nodosus, resolved a 26 µm pigment wavelength. | That the Schnakenberg rates were measured in a Ceratites mantle. | That Boyajian and Lutz reported D_f = 1.34 for this specimen. | That the 1.088 mm sibling slot's diffusivities were reused.]; other sources: Evolution of biological complexity and its relation to taxonomic longevity in the Ammonoidea <https://doi.org/10.1130/0091-7613(1992)020%3C0983:EOBCAI%3E2.3.CO;2>; phenomenon_size: 22 µm (2.176e-05 m, log10=-4.662 m, tier=4_cellular_10um); species_scale_ladder (10 runs from 22 µm to 4.9 cm): 22 µm:ceratites-nodosus-bioelectric-polarity, 68 µm:ceratites-nodosus-vertex-packing, 1.1 mm:ceratites-nodosus-turing-wavelength, 2.2 mm:ceratites-nodosus-clock-wavefront, 4.9 mm:ceratites-nodosus-morphogen-condensation, 6.0 mm:ceratites-nodosus-elastic-buckling, 6.8 mm:ceratites-nodosus-flux-canalization, 9.5 mm:ceratites-nodosus-branching-tree" ;
    pk:derivedFromPaper <https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00289234>,
        <https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-5193(79)90042-0>,
        <https://doi.org/10.1016/s0022-5193(87)80101-7>,
